"Delocalisation and continuity in 2D: loop O(2), six-vertex, and random-cluster models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01527)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Authors: Alexander Glazman, Piet Lammers ... We prove the existence of macroscopic loops in the loop O(2) model with $\\frac12\\leq x^2\\leq 1$ or, equivalently, delocalisation of the associated integer-valued Lipschitz function on the triangular lattice. ... Our approach goes through a novel FKG property required for the non-coexistence theorem of Zhang and Sheffield, which is used to prove delocalisation all the way up to the critical point.\nTitle: Delocalisation and continuity in 2D: loop O(2), six-vertex, and random-cluster models\nAuthors: Alexander Glazman, Piet Lammers\nDate: Fri Jun  2 13:27:12 2023\n\nWe prove the existence of macroscopic loops in the loop O(2) model with $\\frac12\\leq x^2\\leq 1$ or, equivalently, delocalisation of the associated integer-valued Lipschitz function on the triangular lattice. This settles one side of the conjecture of Fan, Domany, and Nienhuis (1970s-80s) that $x^2 = \\frac12$ is the critical point. We also prove delocalisation in the six-vertex model with $0<a,\\,b\\leq c\\leq a+b$. This yields a new proof of continuity of the phase transition in the random-cluster and Potts models in two dimensions for $1\\leq q\\leq 4$ relying neither on integrability tools (parafermionic observables, Bethe Ansatz), nor on the Russo-Seymour-Welsh theory. Our approach goes through a novel FKG property required for the non-coexistence theorem of Zhang and Sheffield, which is used to prove delocalisation all the way up to the critical point. We also use the $\\mathbb T$-circuit argument in the case of the six-vertex model. Finally, we extend an existing renormalisation inequality in order to quantify the delocalisation as being logarithmic, in the regimes $\\frac12\\leq x^2\\leq 1$ and $a=b\\leq c\\leq a+b$. This is consistent with the conjecture that the scaling limit is the Gaussian free field.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDELOCALISATION AND CONTINUITY IN 2D: LOOP O(2), (https://www.pietlammers.com/preprint.delocalisation_continuity_2D.2023-06-02.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; ALEXANDER GLAZMAN AND PIET LAMMERS ... coexistence theorem of Zhang and Sheﬃeld and prove delocalisation all the way up to ... Delocalisation in the loop O(2) model. ... lines of an integer-valued Lipschitz function on the dual triangular lattice. ... 2020 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation.\nDELOCALISATION AND CONTINUITY IN 2D: LOOP O(2),\nSIX-VERTEX, AND RANDOM-CLUSTER MODELS\nALEXANDER GLAZMAN AND PIET LAMMERS\nAbstract. We prove the existence of macroscopic loops in the loop O(2) model with\n1\n2 ≤x2 ≤1. This implies a logarithmic delocalisation of an integer-valued Lipschitz\nfunction on the triangular lattice and settles one side of the conjecture of Fan, Domany,\nand Nienhuis: they predicted in the 1970s–80s that x2 = 1\n2 is the critical point for the\nlocalisation-delocalisation transition.\nWe also prove delocalisation in the six-vertex model with 0 < a, b ≤c ≤a + b.\nThis yields a new proof of continuity of the phase transition in the random-cluster and\nPotts models in two dimensions, for 1 ≤q ≤4. We rely neither on integrability tools\n(parafermionic observables, Bethe Ansatz), nor on the Russo–Seymour–Welsh theory.\nOur approach goes through novel graphical representations that satisfy planar duality,\nthe joint FKG and the domain Markov properties. This enables us to use the non-\ncoexistence theorem of Zhang and Sheﬃeld and prove delocalisation all the way up to\nthe critical point. In the six-vertex model, we additionally use the T-circuit argument.\nFinally, in the regimes 1\n2 ≤x2 ≤1 and a = b ≤c ≤a+b, we use additional symmetries\nto extend existing renormalisation inequalities and establish logarithmic ﬂuctuations of\nthe height at a given point. This is consistent with the conjecture that the scaling limit\nof these height functions is the Gaussian free ﬁeld.\nContents\n1.\nIntroduction\n1\n2.\nDeﬁnitions and formal statements\n8\n3.\nDelocalisation of Lipschitz functions on the triangular lattice\n13\n4.\nDelocalisation of graph homomorphisms in the six-vertex model\n29\n5.\nContinuity of the phase transition in the random-cluster model\n37\nReferences\n44\n1. Introduction\n1.1. Informal statement of the main results. Our results concern three models on\ntwo-dimensional lattices: the loop O(n) model, the six-vertex model, and the FK percolation\nor random-cluster model.\n1.1.1. Delocalisation in the loop O(2) model. The loop O(n) model is a model supported\non loop conﬁgurations on the hexagonal lattice (Fig. 1) and has two real parameters: a\nloop weight n > 0 and an edge weight x > 0. At n = 2, these loops can be viewed as level\nlines of an integer-valued Lipschitz function on the dual triangular lattice. We prove that\nthis height function delocalises whenever 1\n2 ≤x2 ≤1: the variance of the height at a given\nvertex tends to inﬁnity as boundary conditions are taken further and further away; on\nthe loop side, this means that the number of loops surrounding a ﬁxed hexagon tends to\nDate: 2 June 2023.\n2020 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary 82B20, 82B41; secondary 82B30.\nKey words and phrases. Loop O(2) model, six-vertex model, random-cluster model, delocalisation,\ncontinuous phase transition.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContour lines of the two-dimensional discrete Gaussian free field (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605337)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.4 years ago; Authors: Oded Schramm, Scott Sheffield ... We prove that the chordal contour lines of the discrete Gaussian free field converge to forms of SLE(4).Specifically, there is a constant lambda > 0 such that when h is an interpolation of the discrete Gaussian free field on a Jordan domain -- with boundary values -lambda on one boundary arc and lambda on the complementary arc -- the zero level line of h joining the endpoints of these arcs converges to SLE(4) as the domain grows larger.\nTitle: Contour lines of the two-dimensional discrete Gaussian free field\nAuthors: Oded Schramm, Scott Sheffield\nDate: Fri May 12 14:12:39 2006\n\nWe prove that the chordal contour lines of the discrete Gaussian free field converge to forms of SLE(4). Specifically, there is a constant lambda > 0 such that when h is an interpolation of the discrete Gaussian free field on a Jordan domain -- with boundary values -lambda on one boundary arc and lambda on the complementary arc -- the zero level line of h joining the endpoints of these arcs converges to SLE(4) as the domain grows larger. If instead the boundary values are -a < 0 on the first arc and b > 0 on the complementary arc, then the convergence is to SLE(4;a/lambda-1,b/lambda-1), a variant of SLE(4).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA contour line of the continuum Gaussian free field (https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.2447)\nciteturn2academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.2 years ago; Title: A contour line of the continuum Gaussian free fieldAuthors: Oded Schramm, Scott Sheffield ... We argue that even though $h$ is defined only as a random distribution, and not as a function, it has a well-defined zero contour line connecting the endpoints of these arcs, whose law is SLE(4).\nTitle: A contour line of the continuum Gaussian free field\nAuthors: Oded Schramm, Scott Sheffield\nDate: Sat Aug 14 13:54:21 2010\n\nConsider an instance $h$ of the Gaussian free field on a simply connected planar domain with boundary conditions $-\\lambda$ on one boundary arc and $\\lambda$ on the complementary arc, where $\\lambda$ is the special constant $\\sqrt{\\pi/8}$. We argue that even though $h$ is defined only as a random distribution, and not as a function, it has a well-defined zero contour line connecting the endpoints of these arcs, whose law is SLE(4). We construct this contour line in two ways: as the limit of the chordal zero contour lines of the projections of $h$ onto certain spaces of piecewise linear functions, and as the only path-valued function on the space of distributions with a natural Markov property.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelocalisation and Continuity in 2D: Loop $$\\textrm{O}(2)$$, Six-Vertex, and Random-Cluster Models (https://openalex.org/W4409345992)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Year: 2025 ...   * Alexander Glazman (ORCID) — Universität Innsbruck (ROR)  * Piet Lammers (ORCID) — Institut des Hautes Études Scientifiques (ROR) ... Abstract We prove the existence of macroscopic loops in the loop $$\\textrm{O}(2)$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mrow> <mml:mtext>O</mml:mtext> <mml:mo>(</mml:mo> <mml:mn>2</mml:mn> <mml:mo>)</mml:mo> </mml:mrow> </mml:math> model with $$\\frac{1}{2}\\le x^2\\le 1$$ <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mml:mrow> <mml:mfrac> <mml:mn>1</mml:mn> <mml:mn>2</mml:mn> </mml:mfrac> <mml:mo>≤</mml:mo> <mml:msup> <mml:mi>x</mml:mi> <mml:mn>2</mml:mn> </mml:msup> <mml:mo>≤</mml:mo> <mml:mn>1</mml:mn> </mml:mrow> </mml:math> or, equivalently, delocalisation of the associated integer-valued Lipschitz function on the triangular lattice.\n# Delocalisation and Continuity in 2D: Loop $$\\textrm{O}(2)$$, Six-Vertex, and Random-Cluster Models\n\nOpenAlex ID: W4409345992\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s00220-025-05259-9\n\nPMID: 40225196\n\nType: article\n\nYear: 2025\n\nPublication date: 2025-04-10\n\nLanguage: lv\n\n## Source\n\nName: Communications in Mathematical Physics\n\nISSN-L: 0010-3616\n\nISSN: 0010-3616, 1432-0916\n\nPublisher: Springer Science+Business Media\n\n## Citation\n\nVolume: 406\n\nIssue: 5\n\nPages: 108\n\n